 "literal": "Like a red goat among white sheep / while young men like him remain / my young son who stretched out his eating bowl to many / laying him on a bed with his feet aligned / they did not cover him with a pure white shroud!",
 "translation": "Like a single red goat standing out among white sheep, while other young men like him still remain alive, my young boy—who used to stretch out his bowl to many—has not been laid out on a funeral bed with his feet aligned, nor has he been covered with a pure white shroud.",
 "modern": "வெள்ளை ஆடுகளுக்கு இடையே உள்ள சிவப்பு ஆட்டைப் போல, தன்னை ஒத்த மற்ற இளைஞர்கள் உயிரோடு இருக்க, பலரிடமும் தன் உணவுக் கலத்தை நீட்டி இரந்துண்ட என் சிறு மகனை, கால்களை நேரே நீட்டிப் படுக்கையில் கிடத்தி, தூய வெள்ளை ஆடையால் போர்த்தவில்லையே!",
 "explanation": "This poem is a poignant lament by a mother mourning the heroic death of her young son on the battlefield. The mother contrasts her son's unique bravery with his peers, comparing him to a single red goat standing out among a flock of white sheep. She remembers him as a young boy who used to stretch out his bowl to others, highlighting his innocence and youth. Instead of receiving a peaceful domestic funeral where he would be laid on a bed with his feet aligned and covered in a pure white shroud, he lies gloriously but tragically on the battlefield. This belongs to the karantai tiṇai, which celebrates the courage of young warriors who die defending their community's cattle and honor.",
